INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('6b603b26cf223d54e4f5','Renal &Urology amedex 2025.pdf','27','Renal/Urology','A 65-year-old man presents to your practice with a complaint of blood in his urine. These episodes of hematuria have all been
painless. Which one of the following is the least likely cause of painless hematuria?','{"A":"Cancer within the kidney.","B":"Anticoagulation therapy.","C":"Glomerulonephritis.","D":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH).\n¥ OE. Use of cyclophosphamide.\n\nCorrect\nNo data available now.\n\nExplanation:\nCorrect Answer Is E\n\nAll the given options can present with painless hematuria except cyclophosphamide. Cyclophosphamide use can result in\nhemorrhagic cystitis, and with cystitis, the hematuria is more likely to be painful rather than painless.\n\nThe following can result in hematuria (painless or painful):","E":"Urothelial cancers (kidney cancers, bladder cancers, etc)\n¢ Hydronephrosis/distention\n\n¢ Renal vein thrombus / renal artery embolism\n\ne Arteriovenous malformation\n\n¢ Papillary necrosis (sickle cell disease)\n\n¢ Hypertension\n\n¢ Glomerulonephritis\n\ne Structural abnormalities (polycystic kidney disease, medullary sponge kidney, etc)\n¢ Nephrolithiasis\n\ne Urinary tract infections (pyelonephritis, cystitis, parasitic infections, etc)\n¢ Ureteral strictures\n\ne BPH\n\n¢ Prostate cancer\n\ne Prostatic procedures\n\n¢ Trauma (including traumatic catheterization)\n\ne Exercise-induced hematuria\n\n¢ Bleeding diathesis/anticoagulation\n\ne Urethritis\n\n¢ Urethral diverticulum\n\n¢ Hypercalciuria/hyperuricosuria\n\ne Urinary tract fistulas\n\nMimics of hematuria:\n\n¢ Menstruation\n\ne Drugs (phenazopyridine, pygidium, rifampin, nitrofurantoin, etc)\n¢ Pigmenturia\n\n¢ Beeturia"}','E','All the given options can present with painless hematuria except cyclophosphamide. Cyclophosphamide use can result in
hemorrhagic cystitis, and with cystitis, the hematuria is more likely to be painful rather than painless.

The following can result in hematuria (painless or painful):

e Urothelial cancers (kidney cancers, bladder cancers, etc)
¢ Hydronephrosis/distention

¢ Renal vein thrombus / renal artery embolism

e Arteriovenous malformation

¢ Papillary necrosis (sickle cell disease)

¢ Hypertension

¢ Glomerulonephritis

e Structural abnormalities (polycystic kidney disease, medullary sponge kidney, etc)
¢ Nephrolithiasis

e Urinary tract infections (pyelonephritis, cystitis, parasitic infections, etc)
¢ Ureteral strictures

e BPH

¢ Prostate cancer

e Prostatic procedures

¢ Trauma (including traumatic catheterization)

e Exercise-induced hematuria

¢ Bleeding diathesis/anticoagulation

e Urethritis

¢ Urethral diverticulum

¢ Hypercalciuria/hyperuricosuria

e Urinary tract fistulas

Mimics of hematuria:

¢ Menstruation

e Drugs (phenazopyridine, pygidium, rifampin, nitrofurantoin, etc)
¢ Pigmenturia

